# Example 6: finite-nuclear-size effects in hydrogen-like ions

This example shows how RWFS treats the finite size of the nucleus and
reproduces the finite-nuclear-size table of the RWFS paper (Table 4). 
One-electron ions (H, He+, C5+, Ar17+, La56+, Au78+, Db104+) are 
calculated with three nuclear models and the orbital energies are compared.

| Model | RWFS potential option | Input needed |
|---|---|---|
| 1- Point nucleus (Coulomb) | 2 | none |
| 2- Uniformly charged sphere | 8 | nuclear mass number A, or R_rms (fm) |
| 3- Fermi charge distribution | 9 | A or R_rms (fm); 
| skin thickness t (fm), default 2.3 |

## Nuclear radii

When a mass number A is given, RWFS uses the global fit

    R_rms = 0.836 A^(1/3) + 0.570 fm (Johnson and Soff; valid for A > 9)

with A = 12, 40, 139, 197, 268 for C, Ar, La, Au, Db. The radii are
illustrative; use measured values for your isotope. H and He are special:

## Input format

Every batch input file contains, one answer per line: Z, number of electrons (1),
orbital-input mode (1), orbital list, "change default parameters" (n), the
potential option, and the potential parameters. Example, Au78+ with the Fermi
model, A = 197, default t:

    79
    1
    1
    1s 2s 2p- 2p 3d- 3d 4f- 4f
    n
    9
    197
    (empty line = default t = 2.3 fm)

To give R_rms directly, leave the mass-number line **empty** and 
type R_rms in fm on the next line. Empty lines are significant.

## Running

    python src/rwfs.py < examples/example_6/input-au-fermi
